A||||4||four50four||why bring the fiero back?||||13||
Z||000000||four50four||11-25-1999||05:55 PM||||well, theres been a lot of talk lately about bringing the fiero back. I am against bringing it back. Why? Well, the fiero was discontinued in 1988, there have been a lot of tecnical advancements from then, so really, this new car will not have much of a relationship with our fiero.  The car won't be named the fiero, due to a bad reputation, so it will end up called the Pegasus or something else that i don't like. Chances are, it will just be a small, front-engine, front wheel drive, 4 seater, begging to be riced up, and driven by middle-class white 16 year olds, with their hats sideways and rap blasting out of every crevice. It won't be the high-perfromance machine we are used to, because there is no way GM will allow anything that will rival the vette. People will say, "why spend 50 000 on a vette, when we could lose a bit of perforamce, but save $25 000". What if this new car has a severe defect, say all the brakes are faulty. people will automatically assume our fieros also have faulty brakes, and there goes a whole new rumour. Creating a new fiero will change the entire reputation, people will stop looking at each "old" fiero that passes by, and only look at the new ones. Ours won't be the only american mid-engine car anymore. they won't be special. I'd imagine that it will decrease our fieros value.  Developing a mid-engine car is not easy, what did it cost back in the 80's? something like 3 000 000 000. That was the whole point of the fiero, to be a cheap car, that stood out. if pontiac just up and decided to spend this kind of money on a new model, i would be amazed, as the are already in the process of bringing back the GTO, and creating a whole new animal, the Aztek. i doubt there is a huge demand anyway for ANOTHER mid-engine car. people have all been there, done that, and to most of the public, they just set on fire. There is just not enough interest to bring it back. The fiero is over and done with. i know this just sounds like a bunch of pointless babbling, but does anyone feel the same way? ||||reg||13
Z||000001||87GT||11-25-1999||06:03 PM||||I have seen the Aztek and I think its the ugliest thing to role of the assembly line since the Ford Pinto. (no offense to Pinto owners).  I was actually surprised when I looked at the original sticker price for my 87GT.  I expected it to be around the 10,000 price range and it was actually, 15,995.  I think that is an enormous price for a 87 vehicle.  I dont know, maybe I am way wrong.  I do agree, the Fiero will never be back. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||four50four||11-25-1999||06:12 PM||||yeah, the aztek is BUTT-UGLY!!!||||reg||6
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||11-25-1999||11:55 PM||||It is very interesting that we would be pasionate about the whole concept of the return of the Fiero in the light of the MR2 return. The new MR2's focus was and is much more definitive than the Fiero when it first came out. It has a target, the Miata this time, and knows that it is a sports car first. NO pretensions of being a "sporty" commuter car. Can GM actually pull their heads of their collective accountant's nether regions? We anxiously await the answer.<BR>      It is still not in concrete that this "Fiero" will be Pontiac. The Fieros closest living relatives (plastic panels over a steel space frame) are the Saturns. The rumors still persist that Pontiac is not the sure thing even if the Firebird is shot down. Sure it will be called something else but if it talks like a duck and walks like a duck....?<BR>     I seriously doubt with all due respect that a new Fiero would devalue current Fieros anymore than they are. With the reputations that all Fieros have (fires, cant get to the back spark plugs, and more fires) I doubt that anything below an 88 could be devalued anymore than they are already as compared to the MR2s. So why bring the Fiero back if its doomed to be tied down with a possible reputation and a corporate restraining order to stay away from the Corvette's territory? It would be for something that the General Motors Corporation seems to have no concept of. <BR>Corporate Pride. Build the Fiero the way it was supposed to be built and DOMINATE the market. Give it the same chances the Corvette got when its numbers were in the gutter the first few years.<P>Yeah right, the day after the Angels win the penant. (I wish they would prove me wrong...)<P>PS I love this place where I can rant and rave!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Gokart||11-26-1999||07:54 PM||||Well said guys! I've seen a few petitions on different sites to "bring back the Fiero". I wouldn't think of it. Though it would be interesting to see what it may look like, but only if it were mid-engine and made by Pontiac. Our cars are very special, and even though they may not decrease in money value if they were brought back, part of what makes our cars so unique would perish.||||reg||11
